# MONITORING-LOG-SOURCE-RESILIENCE-1 — log source authority

[113-monitoring-logs-watch-truthfulness](113-monitoring-logs-watch-truthfulness.mdc)
established that the logs section may only report OK when it actually read a log and
actually aged its events. It left one assumption standing: that the log is at
`storage/logs/laravel.log`.

That was true only by coincidence. Production resolves `stack -> [single] -> laravel.log`,
so a hardcoded path happened to hit the right file. Change `LOG_CHANNEL` to `daily`, or add
a member to `LOG_STACK`, and the application writes its errors somewhere else while the
monitor keeps reading a file that has stopped moving — and reports OK for a system that is
actively failing. Rule 113 R2 forbids reporting OK without evidence; this rule fixes the
case where the evidence was real but came from the wrong file.

## R1 — The effective logging configuration is the only source authority

The set of files Monitoring reads is derived from `config('logging.*')` via
`App\Services\Monitoring\MonitoringLogSourceResolver`. A hardcoded `laravel.log` is not
sufficient authority and must not be reintroduced — in either monitor, in config, or in a
test fixture.

There is exactly **one** resolver, shared by `PilotPerformanceSnapshotService` and
`FoundationMonitoringStatusService`. Two monitors that disagree about where the application
logs will eventually disagree about whether it is healthy.

A **second** path knob is worse than none. `config/foundation_monitoring.php` used to carry
its own `paths.laravel_log`; it was retired, because a declared path that disagrees with the
running logger is the one the monitor would actually read, and it would win silently. Do not
add one back.

## R2 — A stack resolves to its real members

For a `stack` channel, resolve the configured `channels` — recursively, since Laravel allows
stacks of stacks, with a cycle and depth guard so resolution always terminates. Never assume
`stack == single`, and never treat the shipped `LOG_STACK=single` default as runtime logic.

Every supported member is scanned. The worst finding across members survives the fold; a
healthy sibling never averages away a failing one.

## R3 — Rotation is covered across the whole window, not just today

For `daily`, resolve every day-file whose day intersects the lookback window, using Monolog's
`{filename}-{date}` convention applied to the *configured* path (never an assumed
`laravel.log`), with day boundaries in `config('app.timezone')` — the same clock Monolog
stamps filenames with.

A 24h window read at 00:00:30 spans two calendar days, and today's file covers thirty seconds
of it. Scanning only the current day is how a monitor reports a clean bill of health minutes
after midnight while yesterday's file still holds a live error inside the window.

## R4 — Full-window coverage is required before OK

Generalises 113 R5 from one file to a source set. The logs section may report OK only when
every required source was read back as far as it is responsible for — the window cutoff for
a `single` file, the later of the cutoff and its own day-start for a day-file.

If coverage cannot be proven, the verdict is not OK. `source_coverage_complete` carries this,
and it is false whenever any required source was unresolved, unsupported, missing (except as
allowed by R5), unreadable, or truncated short of its required reach.

## R5 — Unresolvable, missing and unreadable sources fail closed

- **No supported source configured at all** (e.g. the default channel is `syslog`) → the
  monitor has nothing to observe → not OK.
- **Nothing readable was actually read** → not OK. Absence of evidence is never evidence of
  absence.
- **An unsupported member of a monitored stack** (syslog, stderr, Slack, a custom monolog
  handler) → reported, never silently dropped → coverage incomplete → not OK. Omitting it
  is exactly how a partial scan gets presented as a full one.
- **A `single` file that is missing** → not OK. A `single` file is never rotated away, so its
  absence means it was removed or the channel moved — the precise drift this rule exists to
  catch.
- **A present file that cannot be opened** → not OK, with no fabricated byte count.

  **Supersession.** MONITORING-LOGS-WATCH-ROOT-CAUSE-1 scored a missing log file as OK plus a
  warning, reasoning that Laravel creates it on first write so absence usually means nothing
  was logged. That reasoning cannot distinguish a quiet system from a relocated log, and the
  relocated case is the dangerous one. Absence is now scored as what it is: unverified. A
  quiet fresh checkout reporting WATCH is the accepted cost, and it is honest — Monitoring GO
  has never meant Monitoring green.

- **The one tolerated absence**: a `daily` day-file may be missing without breaking coverage
  when its directory was successfully listed *and* the day is still inside the channel's own
  `days` retention horizon — nothing could have removed it, so "not there" is an observation
  that the day was empty rather than a blind spot. Outside retention, or with an unlistable
  directory, absence proves nothing and fails closed.

A `null` channel is exempt from all of the above because it provably keeps nothing. Detect it
by its handler class (`Monolog\Handler\NullHandler`), never by the channel being *named*
`null` — the shipped `null` channel's driver is `monolog`.

## R6 — Configuration is the runtime authority, including when cached

Resolve through `config()`, never `env()`. Production runs with `config:cache`, where `env()`
is not the runtime authority; a resolver reading `env()` would disagree with the running
application in exactly the deployment that matters.

## R7 — Monitored paths come only from trusted config

Log paths are never taken from request input, and no route or CLI option accepts one. The
`log_path` seam on `PilotPerformanceSnapshotService::collect()` is internal and test-only;
it must not be exposed to HTTP or given a CLI flag.

## R8 — Counting across sources may overstate severity, never understate it

Counts are summed across sources. A stack writing the same record to two files therefore
counts it twice, which can only push a verdict *worse*, never green. Do not add
deduplication without evidence that a real configuration double-counts in a way that changes
an operational decision — and never replace the sum with a max, which would let a burst split
across two files be demoted.

## R9 — The metric schema is stable across verdicts

The logs payload is a contract for dashboards and alerts. Even when nothing could be read,
every metric key is emitted, with `null` for anything unmeasured. A verdict that silently
drops half the schema is harder to alert on than one that says "unknown" in each field.
